@node Lexical Analysis
@section Lexical Analysis

The Fortran lexer (@code{fortran-lex-source}) converts the input character
stream into a sequence of tokens. It recognizes keywords, operators,
literals, and identifiers.

@subheading Keywords

Keywords are case-insensitive. The following keywords are recognized:

@table @asis
@item @code{PROGRAM} - Program unit
@item @code{SUBROUTINE} - Subroutine definition
@item @code{FUNCTION} - Function definition
@item @code{END} - End of program unit
@item @code{RETURN} - Return from subroutine
@item @code{STOP} - Terminate execution
@item @code{CALL} - Subroutine call
@item @code{GOTO} - Unconditional transfer
@item @code{IF} - Conditional
@item @code{THEN} - THEN clause (optional in some forms)
@item @code{ELSE} - ELSE clause
@item @code{DO} - DO loop
@item @code{READ} - Input statement
@item @code{WRITE} - Output statement
@item @code{PRINT} - Print statement
@item @code{OPEN} - Open file (stub)
@item @code{CLOSE} - Close file (stub)
@item @code{REAL} - Real type
@item @code{INTEGER} - Integer type
@item @code{LOGICAL} - Logical type
@item @code{CHARACTER} - Character type
@item @code{COMPLEX} - Complex type
@item @code{DATA} - Data initialization (stub)
@item @code{PARAMETER} - Named constant (stub)
@item @code{DIMENSION} - Array declaration (stub)
@item @code{COMMON} - Common block (stub)
@item @code{EQUIVALENCE} - Equivalence statement (stub)
@item @code{INCLUDE} - Include file (stub)
@item @code{COPY} - Copybook inclusion
@item @code{WHILE} - While loop
@item @code{UNTIL} - Until loop
@item @code{AND} - Logical AND
@item @code{OR} - Logical OR
@item @code{NOT} - Logical NOT
@item @code{TRUE} - Logical true
@item @code{FALSE} - Logical false
@end table

@subheading Object-Oriented Keywords

@table @asis
@item @code{CLASS} - Class definition
@item @code{METHOD} - Method definition
@item @code{INSTANCE} - Instance creation
@item @code{NEW} - Create new instance
@item @code{DELETE} - Delete instance (stub)
@item @code{INVOKE} - Method invocation
@item @code{SUPER} - Superclass reference
@item @code{SELF} - Current instance reference
@item @code{PROCEDURE} - Procedure declaration
@end table

@subheading Operators

@table @asis
@item @code{+} - Addition
@item @code{-} - Subtraction or negation
@item @code{*} - Multiplication
@item @code{/} - Division
@item @code{**} - Exponentiation (unsupported)
@item @code{=} - Assignment or equality
@item @code{==} - Equality comparison
@item @code{/=} - Inequality
@item @code{<>} - Inequality (alternate)
@item @code{<} - Less than
@item @code{>} - Greater than
@item @code{<=} - Less than or equal
@item @code{>=} - Greater than or equal
@item @code{( )} - Parentheses for grouping
@item @code{,} - Argument separator
@item @code{:} - Range separator
@item @code{.} - Field separator
@end table

@subheading Lines Beginning with @code{C} or @code{*}

Lines beginning with @code{C} or @code{*} in column 1 are treated as
comment lines and ignored by the lexer.

@node Number Literal Formats
@section Number Literal Formats

The Fortran frontend supports multiple number literal formats for specifying
integer values in different bases.

@subheading Decimal Format

Standard base-10 integer notation:

@example
123
0
255
65535
@end example

@subheading Hexadecimal Format (Modern)

Prefix @code{0X} or @code{0x} followed by hex digits:

@example
0XFF         @r{= 255}
0XDEADBEEF   @r{= 3735928559}
0x1A2B       @r{= 6699}
@end example

@subheading Hexadecimal Format (FORTRAN Quoted)

Prefix @code{X'} or @code{x'} with quoted hex digits:

@example
X'FF'        @r{= 255}
X'DEADBEEF'  @r{= 3735928559}
x'1A2B'      @r{= 6699}
@end example

@subheading Binary Format (Modern)

Prefix @code{0B} or @code{0b} followed by binary digits:

@example
0B1010       @r{= 10}
0B11110000   @r{= 240}
0b1          @r{= 1}
@end example

@subheading Binary Format (FORTRAN Quoted)

Prefix @code{B'} or @code{b'} with quoted binary digits:

@example
B'1010'      @r{= 10}
B'11110000'  @r{= 240}
b'1'         @r{= 1}
@end example

@subheading Octal Format (Modern)

Prefix @code{0O} or @code{0o} followed by octal digits:

@example
0O755        @r{= 493}
0O123        @r{= 83}
0o77         @r{= 63}
@end example

@subheading Octal Format (FORTRAN Quoted)

Prefix @code{O'} or @code{o'} with quoted octal digits:

@example
O'755'       @r{= 493}
O'123'       @r{= 83}
o'77'        @r{= 63}
@end example

@subheading Dword Format (Legacy)

Prefix @code{DWORD} followed by decimal value (represents 32-bit value):

@example
DWORD65536   @r{= 65536}
DWORD1000    @r{= 1000}
@end example

@subheading Hexadecimal Format (FORTRAN Legacy)

Prefix @code{D'} or @code{d'} with quoted hex digits:

@example
D'FF'        @r{= 255}
D'DEAD'      @r{= 57005}
@end example

@subheading Summary Table

@table @asis
@item Decimal - @code{123} - Base 10
@item Hex (modern) - @code{0XFF} - Base 16
@item Hex (FORTRAN) - @code{X'FF'} - Base 16
@item Binary (modern) - @code{0B1010} - Base 2
@item Binary (FORTRAN) - @code{B'1010'} - Base 2
@item Octal (modern) - @code{0O755} - Base 8
@item Octal (FORTRAN) - @code{O'755'} - Base 8
@item Dword - @code{DWORD65536} - 32-bit value
@end table

@node Identifier Normalization
@section Identifier Normalization

Identifiers are normalized to PascalCase form for consistent AST generation.

@subheading Rules

@itemize @bullet
@item All lowercase letters are converted to uppercase
@item Underscores are treated as word separators
@item The first letter of each word is capitalized
@item Underscores are removed from the final identifier
@end itemize

@subheading Examples

@table @asis
@item @code{my_variable} - @code{MyVariable}
@item @code{COUNTER} - @code{Counter}
@item @code{x_coord_value} - @code{XCoordValue}
@item @code{player_score_final} - @code{PlayerScoreFinal}
@item @code{ALLCAPS} - @code{Allcaps}
@end table

@node Program Structure
@section Program Structure

A Fortran program consists of a PROGRAM block containing statements.
Subroutines and functions define callable procedures.

@subheading Program Declaration

@example
PROGRAM @var{program-name}
  @var{statements}
END
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
PROGRAM GameLogic
  Score = 0
  Lives = 3
  CALL InitializeGame
  CALL MainLoop
  STOP
END
@end example

@example
PROGRAM Calculator
  X = 10
  Y = 20
  Z = X + Y
  PRINT Z
END
@end example

@subheading Valid Inputs

@itemize @bullet
@item Program name: valid identifier
@item Statements: zero or more statements
@end itemize

@subheading Outputs

@itemize @bullet
@item AST: Program node with embedded MAIN method
@item Method body: list of statement nodes
@end itemize

@node Subroutines and Functions
@section Subroutines and Functions

Subroutines and functions define named blocks of code that can be called
from other parts of the program.

@subheading Subroutine Declaration

@example
SUBROUTINE @var{name}(@var{arguments})
  @var{statements}
END @var{name}
@end example

@subheading Function Declaration

@example
FUNCTION @var{name}(@var{arguments}) @var{return-type}
  @var{statements}
END @var{name}
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
SUBROUTINE InitializeGame()
  Score = 0
  Lives = 3
  CALL LoadAssets
END InitializeGame
@end example

@example
FUNCTION AddNumbers(A, B) INTEGER
  Result = A + B
  RETURN
END AddNumbers
@end example

@subheading Note on Arguments

@itemize @bullet
@item Argument lists are parsed but ignored in the current implementation
@item Return types for functions are parsed but ignored
@item Actual parameter passing is handled by the backend
@end itemize

@subheading Valid Inputs

@itemize @bullet
@item Name: valid identifier
@item Arguments: comma-separated list of identifiers
@item Return type (functions): type specifier
@end itemize

@subheading Outputs

@itemize @bullet
@item AST: Method node with the given name
@end itemize

@node Variable Declarations
@section Variable Declarations

Variable type declarations are parsed but currently generate errors
(unsupported feature).

@subheading Type Specifiers

@example
INTEGER @var{variable-name}
REAL @var{variable-name}
LOGICAL @var{variable-name}
CHARACTER @var{variable-name}
COMPLEX @var{variable-name}
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
INTEGER Count
REAL X, Y, Z
LOGICAL IsActive
CHARACTER Name
@end example

@subheading Note

Type declarations are recognized by the lexer and parser but currently
raise a compile-time error indicating the feature is unsupported. Variable
typing is handled through the EightBol type system instead.

@node Assignment
@section Assignment

Variables are assigned values using the assignment operator (@code{=}).

@subheading Syntax

@example
@var{variable} = @var{expression}
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
Score = 0
Counter = Counter + 1
Total = Price + Tax
IsValid = X > 0 AND Y > 0
Name = "Player1"
@end example

@subheading Valid Inputs

@itemize @bullet
@item Target: variable name
@item Expression: any valid expression
@end itemize

@subheading Outputs

@itemize @bullet
@item AST: @code{(:fortran-move :from @var{expr} :to @var{target})}
@end itemize

@node Control Flow
@section Control Flow

Fortran provides several control flow constructs.

@subheading IF/THEN/ELSE

@example
IF (@var{condition}) THEN
  @var{then-statements}
ELSE
  @var{else-statements}
END IF
@end example

@subsubheading Simple IF

@example
IF (X > 0) THEN
  Y = X
END IF
@end example

@subsubheading IF with ELSE

@example
IF (Score > 100) THEN
  Bonus = Bonus + 10
ELSE
  Bonus = 0
END IF
@end example

@subsubheading Nested IF

@example
IF (Temperature > 100) THEN
  Status = "HOT"
ELSE IF (Temperature > 50) THEN
  Status = "WARM"
ELSE
  Status = "COLD"
END IF
@end example

@subheading GOTO Statement

Unconditional transfer to a labeled statement:

@example
GOTO @var{label}
@end example

@example
IF (ErrorCode /= 0) THEN
  GOTO ErrorHandler
END IF

@r{... normal processing ...}

ErrorHandler:
  CALL HandleError
END
@end example

@subheading DO Loop

Counting loop with optional step:

@example
DO @var{variable} = @var{start}, @var{end}, @var{step}
  @var{statements}
END DO
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
DO I = 1, 10
  Score = Score + 10
END DO
@end example

@example
DO X = 0, 79, 2
  CALL DrawPixel X, Y
END DO
@end example

@example
DO Index = Length, 0, -1
  Items[Index] = 0
END DO
@end example

@subheading Valid Inputs

@itemize @bullet
@item IF condition: boolean expression
@item DO variable: identifier
@item DO start/end: integer expressions
@item DO step: optional integer expression (default: 1)
@end itemize

@subheading Outputs

@itemize @bullet
@item IF: @code{(:fortran-if :condition @var{cond} :then @var{then-stmts} :else @var{else-stmts})}
@item GOTO: @code{(:goto :target @var{label})}
@item DO: @code{(:fortran-do :var @var{var} :from @var{start} :to @var{end} :by @var{step} :stmts @var{stmts})}
@end itemize

@node Procedure Calls
@section Procedure Calls

Procedures are called using the CALL statement.

@subheading Syntax

@example
CALL @var{procedure-name}(@var{arguments})
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
CALL InitializeGame
CALL DrawSprite(SpriteId, X, Y)
CALL PlaySound(MusicTrack)
CALL ProcessData(A, B, C)
@end example

@subheading Valid Inputs

@itemize @bullet
@item Procedure name: valid identifier
@item Arguments: comma-separated list of expressions
@end itemize

@subheading Outputs

@itemize @bullet
@item AST: @code{(:call :target @var{name})}
@end itemize

@node Input and Output
@section Input and Output

Input and output statements provide basic I/O capabilities. These are
currently stubs that generate calls to runtime routines.

@subheading READ Statement

@example
READ @var{unit}, @var{variables}
@end example

@example
READ *, Count
READ(5, *) X, Y, Z
READ(1, FMT=100) Data
@end example

@subheading WRITE Statement

@example
WRITE @var{unit}, @var{format} @var{variables}
@end example

@example
WRITE(*, *) Score
WRITE(6, *) "Total: ", Total
WRITE(1, FMT=100) Buffer
@end example

@subheading PRINT Statement

@example
PRINT @var{format}, @var{variables}
@end example

@example
PRINT *, Score
PRINT *, "Hello, World!"
PRINT 100, X, Y, Z
@end example

@subheading Note

These I/O statements are implemented as stubs that emit calls to runtime
routines (@code{ReadRoutine}, @code{WriteRoutine}, @code{PrintRoutine}).
The actual I/O operations are performed by the runtime library.

@subheading Valid Inputs

@itemize @bullet
@item READ: unit number and variable list
@item WRITE: unit number, format, and variable list
@item PRINT: format specifier and variable list
@end itemize

@subheading Outputs

@itemize @bullet
@item AST: Call nodes to I/O runtime routines
@end itemize

@node Copybook Inclusion
@section Copybook Inclusion

Copybooks are source files containing shared declarations that can be
included in multiple programs.

@subheading Syntax

@example
COPY "@var{filename}"
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
COPY "common/constants.for"
COPY "game/config.for"
COPY "shared/types.for"
@end example

@subheading Valid Inputs

@itemize @bullet
@item Filename: string literal with file path
@end itemize

@subheading Outputs

@itemize @bullet
@item AST: @code{(:copy :name @var{filename})}
@end itemize

@node Object-Oriented Extensions
@section Object-Oriented Extensions

The Fortran frontend includes extensions for object-oriented programming,
inspired by the EightBol OOP system.

@subheading CLASS Definition

Define a class:

@example
CLASS @var{class-name}
END @var{class-name}
@end example

@example
CLASS Player
END Player
@end example

@subheading METHOD Definition

Define a method within a class:

@example
CLASS @var{class-name}
  METHOD @var{method-name}(@var{params}): @var{statements}
END @var{class-name}
@end example

@example
CLASS GameObject
  METHOD Move(DX, DY): X = X + DX, Y = Y + DY
END GameObject
@end example

@subheading INSTANCE Creation

Create an instance of a class:

@example
@var{variable} = NEW @var{class-name}(@var{args})
@end example

@example
Player1 = NEW Player()
Enemy = NEW Enemy(100, 200)
@end example

@subheading INVOKE Method Call

Invoke a method on an object:

@example
INVOKE @var{object}.@var{method}(@var{args})
@end example

@example
INVOKE Player.Move(5, 10)
INVOKE Sprite.Draw(X, Y)
INVOKE GameState.Update
@end example

@subheading SUPER Call

Call the parent class method:

@example
INVOKE SUPER.@var{method}(@var{args})
@end example

@example
INVOKE SUPER.Initialize
INVOKE SUPER.Update(deltaTime)
@end example

@subheading Valid Inputs

@itemize @bullet
@item CLASS: class name identifier
@item METHOD: method name, parameters, and body
@item NEW: class name and optional arguments
@item INVOKE: object name, method name, and optional arguments
@end itemize

@subheading Outputs

@itemize @bullet
@item CLASS: @code{(:fortran-class :name @var{class-name})}
@item METHOD: @code{(:fortran-method :method-name @var{name})}
@item NEW: @code{(:fortran-new :target @var{class} :args @var{args})}
@item INVOKE: @code{(:invoke :object @var{obj} :method @var{method})}
@item SUPER: @code{(:invoke-super :method @var{method})}
@end itemize

@node Arithmetic Operations
@section Arithmetic Operations

The frontend supports standard arithmetic operations.

@subheading Binary Operations

@example
@var{left} + @var{right}       @r{Addition}
@var{left} - @var{right}       @r{Subtraction}
@var{left} * @var{right}       @r{Multiplication}
@var{left} / @var{right}       @r{Division}
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
Total = Base + Tax
Difference = X - Y
Product = Width * Height
Quotient = Dividend / Divisor
@end example

@subheading Parenthesized Expressions

@example
Result = (A + B) * C
Value = ((X - Y) / Z) + 1
@end example

@subheading Valid Inputs

@itemize @bullet
@item Operands: integer or real expressions
@item Division by zero: handled by runtime
@end itemize

@subheading Outputs

@itemize @bullet
@item Addition: @code{(:add :from @var{left} :to @var{right} :giving nil)}
@item Subtraction: @code{(:subtract :subtrahend @var{right} :from @var{left} :giving nil)}
@item Multiplication: @code{(:multiply :by @var{left} :multiplier @var{right} :giving nil)}
@item Division: @code{(:divide :numerator @var{left} :denominator @var{right} :giving nil)}
@end itemize

@node Relational and Logical Operations
@section Relational and Logical Operations

@subheading Relational Operations

@example
@var{left} == @var{right}      @r{Equality}
@var{left} /= @var{right}      @r{Inequality}
@var{left} <> @var{right}      @r{Inequality (alternate)}
@var{left} < @var{right}       @r{Less than}
@var{left} > @var{right}       @r{Greater than}
@var{left} <= @var{right}      @r{Less or equal}
@var{left} >= @var{right}      @r{Greater or equal}
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
IF (X == Y) THEN
IF (Count /= 0) THEN
IF (Value > 0) THEN
IF (Index <= MAX) THEN
@end example

@subheading Logical Operations

@example
@var{a} AND @var{b}             @r{Logical AND}
@var{a} OR @var{b}              @r{Logical OR}
NOT @var{a}                     @r{Logical NOT}
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
IF (X > 0 AND Y > 0) THEN
IF (Valid OR Force) THEN
IF (NOT Done) THEN
@end example

@subheading Valid Inputs

@itemize @bullet
@item Relational: both operands must be comparable types
@item Logical: operands must be boolean expressions
@end itemize

@subheading Outputs

@itemize @bullet
@item Equality: @code{'(= @var{left} @var{right})}
@item Inequality: @code{' (/= @var{left} @var{right})}
@item Less than: @code{' (< @var{left} @var{right})}
@item Greater than: @code{' (> @var{left} @var{right})}
@item Less or equal: @code{' (<= @var{left} @var{right})}
@item Greater or equal: @code{' (>= @var{left} @var{right})}
@item Logical AND: @code{' (:and @var{left} @var{right})}
@item Logical OR: @code{' (:or @var{left} @var{right})}
@item Logical NOT: @code{' (:not @var{left})}
@end itemize

@node Unsupported Features
@section Unsupported Features

The following Fortran features are recognized by the lexer but are not
implemented in the parser. Each generates a compile-time error when
encountered.

@table @asis
@item @strong{DATA} - Data initialization statements
@item @strong{PARAMETER} - Named constant declarations
@item @strong{DIMENSION} - Array declaration statements
@item @strong{COMMON} - Common block declarations
@item @strong{EQUIVALENCE} - Equivalence statements
@item @strong{INCLUDE} - Include file directives
@item @strong{TYPE} - Derived type declarations
@end table

@subheading Error Messages

When an unsupported feature is encountered, the compiler raises a
@code{source-error} with a message indicating which feature is not
supported:

@example
FORTRAN DATA is not supported.
FORTRAN PARAMETER is not supported.
FORTRAN DIMENSION is not supported.
FORTRAN COMMON is not supported.
FORTRAN EQUIVALENCE is not supported.
FORTRAN INCLUDE is not supported.
type declaration is not supported.
@end example

@subheading Alternative Approaches

@itemize @bullet
@item For DATA: Use direct assignment statements
@item For PARAMETER: Use named constants in the EightBol configuration
@item For DIMENSION: Use COPY with array definitions from COBOL copybooks
@item For COMMON: Use shared variables in the EightBol globals system
@item For INCLUDE: Use COPY statement for source file inclusion
@item For TYPE: Use COBOL record definitions via COPY
@end itemize

@subheading Additional Limitations

@table @asis
@item @strong{Exponentiation} - The @code{**} operator raises an unsupported error
@item @strong{Function return values} - Functions do not currently return values
@item @strong{Argument passing} - Argument lists are parsed but not transmitted to the AST
@item @strong{Return types} - Function return types are parsed but not stored
@item @strong{File I/O} - OPEN and CLOSE are stubs
@item @strong{I/O formats} - Format specifications are parsed but ignored
@end table
